1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:03,220
Touch the wooden part of a chair.

2
00:00:03,470 --> 00:00:05,360
Now touch its metal leg.

3
00:00:05,610 --> 00:00:07,230
The wood feels normal.

4
00:00:07,480 --> 00:00:09,200
The metal feels cold.

5
00:00:09,450 --> 00:00:13,580
They’ve been sitting beside each
other all day. No refrigerator.

6
00:00:13,640 --> 00:00:16,070
No air conditioner aimed at the chair.

7
00:00:16,320 --> 00:00:18,450
So why does one feel colder?

8
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:22,070
Here’s the surprise.

9
00:00:22,320 --> 00:00:25,330
Once both have settled to
the same room temperature,

10
00:00:25,450 --> 00:00:28,450
the metal can feel colder
even though it isn’t.

11
00:00:28,700 --> 00:00:31,800
And there’s a way to make
that difference even stranger.

12
00:00:32,050 --> 00:00:34,320
Put an ice cube on each material.

13
00:00:34,570 --> 00:00:36,250
Under suitable conditions,

14
00:00:36,290 --> 00:00:40,430
the metal—the one that feels
colder—can melt its ice faster.

15
00:00:41,080 --> 00:00:45,630
The explanation begins with what happens
when your hand touches something.

16
00:00:45,880 --> 00:00:50,300
Your hand is usually warmer than
the objects in a comfortable room.

17
00:00:50,550 --> 00:00:51,840
When you touch one,

18
00:00:51,910 --> 00:00:56,200
thermal energy transfers from your
warmer skin into the cooler object.

19
00:00:56,450 --> 00:00:58,920
That transfer is called conduction.

20
00:00:59,170 --> 00:01:02,780
But different materials transfer
energy at different rates.

21
00:01:03,030 --> 00:01:08,380
A metal chair leg conducts heat away from
your fingers more readily than wood does.

22
00:01:08,630 --> 00:01:11,600
Your skin cools faster
at the contact point.

23
00:01:11,850 --> 00:01:15,110
You experience that as a
stronger feeling of cold.

24
00:01:15,760 --> 00:01:19,500
Picture the heat leaving your
hand as little moving lights.

25
00:01:19,750 --> 00:01:24,190
On the metal side, the lights spread
quickly away from the place you touch.

26
00:01:24,440 --> 00:01:27,270
On the wood side, they spread more slowly.

27
00:01:27,520 --> 00:01:30,050
These lights are just an illustration.

28
00:01:30,240 --> 00:01:33,400
Heat doesn’t literally
travel as glowing dots.

29
00:01:33,650 --> 00:01:36,220
But the difference they represent is real.

30
00:01:36,470 --> 00:01:38,930
Metal is a good thermal conductor.

31
00:01:39,180 --> 00:01:41,030
Wood is a much poorer one.

32
00:01:41,280 --> 00:01:46,482
Your hand is reacting to that exchange of
energy—not simply reading a temperature

33
00:01:46,522 --> 00:01:47,920
off the surface.

34
00:01:48,570 --> 00:01:51,140
Think about a thermometer
beside your hand.

35
00:01:51,390 --> 00:01:55,460
The thermometer helps answer,
“What temperature is this object?”

36
00:01:55,710 --> 00:01:58,950
The sensation in your
fingers also depends on,

37
00:01:59,080 --> 00:02:03,060
“How quickly is this object changing
the temperature of my skin?”

38
00:02:03,310 --> 00:02:05,480
Those questions are related.

39
00:02:05,730 --> 00:02:07,520
But they aren’t identical.

40
00:02:07,770 --> 00:02:12,471
That’s why two surfaces can produce the
same temperature reading and a different

41
00:02:12,511 --> 00:02:13,930
reaction from you.

42
00:02:14,580 --> 00:02:16,730
Now return to those ice cubes.

43
00:02:16,980 --> 00:02:21,518
We’ll use a substantial metal block and a
wooden block that have both been sitting

44
00:02:21,558 --> 00:02:22,880
in the same room.

45
00:02:23,130 --> 00:02:25,230
Both start warmer than the ice.

46
00:02:25,480 --> 00:02:29,030
This time, energy flows from
the blocks into the ice.

47
00:02:29,280 --> 00:02:32,310
The metal transfers heat
to the ice more readily.

48
00:02:32,560 --> 00:02:35,210
So the ice on the metal can melt faster.

49
00:02:35,460 --> 00:02:40,684
The material that took heat away from your
warm hand quickly can also deliver heat to

50
00:02:40,724 --> 00:02:42,110
cold ice quickly.

51
00:02:42,360 --> 00:02:44,230
There’s no contradiction.

52
00:02:44,480 --> 00:02:47,740
The direction depends on
which object is warmer.

53
00:02:47,990 --> 00:02:52,558
The exact melting speed depends on
the setup: the blocks, their size,

54
00:02:52,598 --> 00:02:56,740
their starting temperatures, and
how well the ice touches them.

55
00:02:56,990 --> 00:03:02,050
Our animation shows the principle.
It isn’t a timed laboratory result.

56
00:03:02,700 --> 00:03:04,920
And there’s another useful twist.

57
00:03:05,170 --> 00:03:07,480
Metal doesn’t always feel colder.

58
00:03:07,730 --> 00:03:11,480
Imagine metal and wood that
are both hotter than your skin.

59
00:03:11,730 --> 00:03:13,880
Now heat flows toward your hand.

60
00:03:14,130 --> 00:03:19,110
The metal can transfer that energy into
your skin faster, making it feel hotter.

61
00:03:19,360 --> 00:03:22,160
The same property works
in both directions.

62
00:03:22,410 --> 00:03:26,080
That’s why “metal feels
cold” is an incomplete rule.

63
00:03:26,330 --> 00:03:29,560
A better rule is that metal
transfers heat readily.

64
00:03:29,810 --> 00:03:34,230
Whether that cools you or heats you
depends on the temperature difference.

65
00:03:34,480 --> 00:03:36,650
One final detail matters.

66
00:03:37,300 --> 00:03:42,460
Two objects being in the same room doesn’t
guarantee they’re at the same temperature.

67
00:03:42,710 --> 00:03:46,810
Sunlight, a nearby heater, or
recent use can change that.

68
00:03:47,060 --> 00:03:51,179
Our comparison assumes the objects
have had time to settle to the same

69
00:03:51,219 --> 00:03:54,540
temperature, away from those influences.

70
00:03:54,790 --> 00:03:59,461
Under those conditions, the difference
you feel can come from how the materials

71
00:03:59,501 --> 00:04:01,390
exchange heat with your skin.

72
00:04:01,640 --> 00:04:05,700
So next time a metal chair leg
feels colder than its wooden seat,

73
00:04:05,810 --> 00:04:08,480
remember what your hand is experiencing.

74
00:04:09,130 --> 00:04:11,370
The metal may not have started colder.

75
00:04:11,620 --> 00:04:14,140
It’s simply carrying
your heat away faster.

76
00:04:14,390 --> 00:04:15,570
Same room.

77
00:04:15,820 --> 00:04:17,680
Same starting temperature.

78
00:04:17,930 --> 00:04:19,560
Different experience.

79
00:04:20,210 --> 00:04:21,420
Now you know.

80
00:04:21,670 --> 00:04:23,520
Ordinary has secrets.
